South Africa’s quarterly GDP statistics are subject to an 85-day publication lag, which creates significant information gaps for real-time economic analysis and decision-making. Nowcasting addresses this challenge by estimating current-quarter GDP growth using higher-frequency monthly indicators such as retail sales, PMI, and electricity usage through purpose-built econometric models. This paper develops a comprehensive nowcasting framework for South African quarterly GDP year-over-year (YoY) growth using 19 mixed-frequency indicators spanning 1999Q1 through 2025Q4.
The empirical strategy employs a model suite comprising autoregressive AR(1,4) benchmarks, ARMA(1,0,2) specifications with lags determined by autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation function analysis, pooled indicator regressions, vector autoregressions, mixed-data sampling regressions, a five-factor dynamic factor model, LASSO regularization, and principal surrogate factor models, augmented by a structural dummy variable equal to unity from 2016Q1 onwards. Recursive step-ahead forecasts are generated using an initial training period of 1999Q1–2021Q4 to predict 2022Q1, with subsequent forecasts produced through four-quarter rolling window expansions. The forecast combination, optimally weighted by inverse rolling-window mean squared errors, delivers superior nowcast accuracy by systematically exploiting complementary model strengths while mitigating individual model biases.


